As the filing period draws to a close for the ABC and Bellflower unified school district boards, two incumbents will not run for re-election.
Incumbents James Kang, of ABC, and Donald McMackin, of Bellflower, chose not to seek re-election. As a result, the filing deadline for challengers was extended from 5 p.m. Friday to 5 p.m. today.
Each of those districts has three seats up for election; the other two incumbents in each race have decided to seek re-election.
Paramount and Downey unified school districts also are scheduled to have elections in November.
In Paramount, incumbents Sonya S. Cuellar, Alicia M. Linden Anderson and Tony Pena don��t have any challengers. If the incumbents are confirmed as qualified candidates, the school board will have to decide if it wants to hold the election or cancel it through a vote.
Unlike the other districts, Downey operates a seven-district, by-area voting system, meaning the district is divided into seven zones with representatives elected from each of them.
Last August, the school board abolished its at-large voting system in favor of the district one. In an at-large voting system, candidates can reside in any area of the district, and any eligible voter may vote for any candidate, regardless of where that voter resides.
The school board abandoned the at-large voting system after concerns surfaced about it violated the California Voting Rights Act of 2001 by disenfranchising minority voters.
