The state will spend $65.1 million over a 15-year period starting in 2019 to maintain and operate public access amenities at Gulf State Park, according to a settlement agreement reached Thursday.
The settlement was reached in a 2014 federal lawsuit questioning the use of BP oil spill money to construct a beachside hotel and conference center between Gulf Shores and Orange Beach. The $85.5 million portion of the overall Gulf State Park renovation project is anchored by a Hilton hotel and is expected to be constructed by the fall 2018.
"This settlement guarantees that public dollars will go to ensuring better public access to Alabama beaches," said attorney Robert Wiygul, who represents New Orleans-based Gulf Restoration Network, which sued multiple state and federal agencies objecting to the original allocation of $58.5 million for the project.
"This is a huge win for Alabama," said Cynthia Sarthou, executive director at GRN. "At a time when the state is closing parks and cutting services, this settlement guarantees that BP disaster funds will go towards providing free beach and park access to Alabama residents for the next 20 years."
Chris Blankenship, commissioner with the Alabama Department of Conservation and Natural Resources, said the state is "very happy we reached an amicable settlement" with GNR. He said the state had planned, all along, to oversee the operations and maintenance of the public areas.
"This just memorializes it in a settlement agreement and ensures those who come after us will operate those facilities to the standards we expect to operate them in," he said.
The agreement stipulates that the state will maintain and manage a host of non-conference center elements of the project for 20 years, and will not charge fees for the public's use.
Among the features the state will be responsible for include: A research and education facility, trails, dune enhancement, an interpretive lobby, public restrooms and post-beach showers, public education programs, public beach access from the conference center, a tram system, a pedestrian path, bicycle share stations and programs and a meeting space view area.
The agreement also prohibits the state, or any agency, from building a north-south connecting road through Gulf State Park for the next 20 years.
Hank Caddell, a Mobile attorney who is also the vice chairman of the GRN board, praised that portion of the settlement, saying it was "strong for the public and the environment."
"This is a wonderful natural area," Caddell said. "If you put a three-to-four lane highway through it, you are paving paradise."
Blankenship said revenues generated from the conference center and hotel will pay for the $65.{r}1{r} million{r} project{r}.{g}
