When the Dodgers signed pitcher Scott Kazmir to a three-year, $48 million contract last week, they knew they weren��t getting the young man with a mid-90s fastball and hard slider who once led the American League in strikeouts.
That pitcher, a 31-year-old Scott Kazmir said on a conference call with reporters Tuesday, would ��just get (the ball) and throw it.��
��I don��t know how I did it,�� he said. ��I just did it.��
Thanks to a one-year layoff from organized baseball, Kazmir is acutely aware of how he makes a living throwing a baseball.
The numbers show this much: Kazmir��s fastball averaged 91.3 mph last season, his changeup 76.0 mph. Simple arithmetic shows a 15.3-mph difference between his two favorite pitches, and complicated arithmetic (according to FanGraphs.com) shows that major-league pitchers as a group throw their changeup 8.2 mph slower than their fastballs.
That unusual degree of deception helps make Kazmir just as effective against right-handed hitters as lefties. It��s also allowed him to extend his career three years since he last pitched for the Sugar Land Skeeters, the unaffiliated minor league team that employed Kazmir in 2012.
For Kazmir, transforming his repertoire was more about the process than the end result.
��I wanted to step back, start from scratch, go back to fundamentals, go back to great habits and get away from those bad habits I created,�� he said.
The last time Kazmir was seen pitching for a Southern California team was April 3, 2011. It was a getaway day, a Sunday, and the Angels were wrapping up a series in Kansas City.
Kazmir faced 14 batters that afternoon and recorded five outs. He was charged with five runs in 1 2/3 innings, went on the disabled list the following week, was released by the Angels in June and didn��t pitch another inning in the major leagues for two years, when the new Kazmir resurfaced with the Cleveland Indians.
��It was a long process,�� Kazmir said. ��Definitely a low point, getting released by the Angels at the time.��
