The growth of Marin County property tax revenue — vital to the funding of county government, local school districts and special districts — will continue to slow in 2017-18.
The assessed value of Marin property, on which property taxes are based, will close at $74.2 billion in 2017-18, a 5.3 percent increase from the previous year. In 2016-17 the assessment roll rose 6.3 percent.
It is the second consecutive year of slower growth. In 2015-16 the assessment roll rose 7.11 percent.
County Assessor Richard Benson said there were two chief reasons for the slower growth in assessed value: fewer housing units sold and fewer homeowners had their property taxes readjusted under Proposition 13 rules following the Great Recession.
Benson said the escalation in Marin County real estate prices moderated somewhat this year and the number of properties sold declined slightly. Benson said 2,947 single-family homes were sold in Marin in 2016, 10.4 percent fewer than the 3,289 single-family homes sold in 2015.
The slowdown in assessed value growth was predicted by county budget analysts. In January, county Budget Manager Bret Uppendahl said growth in property tax revenue, one of the county��s biggest revenue sources, had begun to slow.
After plummeting due to the Great Recession, property tax revenue began a steady recovery in 2011-12, peaking at a growth rate of about 7 percent in fiscal 2015-16. Uppendahl said then that he expected the growth rate to hover around 5 percent over the next five years, slightly below its 20-year average.
Partly due to the lower property tax revenue, Uppendahl forecast escalating general fund operating shortfalls beginning in fiscal 2018-19 with a deficit of $4.7 million, and $7.4 million in fiscal 2019-20, $10.6 million in fiscal 2020-21 and $12.7 million in 2021-22.
Property tax revenue accounts for about 30 percent of the county of Marin��s total revenue, about the same share as the county gets in state funding. Historically, Marin��s property tax revenue has grown at an average rate of about 5.4 percent annually — just fast enough to cover 3 percent cost-of-living adjustments for county employees.
Benson reported the average sales price of a single-family residence in Marin in 2016 was $1.47 million, about the same as the previous year. The median sales price was about $1.16 million, up 5.9 percent. The average price for an ��attached�� home, or townhouse or condo, was $649,445, up 2.2 percent, and the median price was $596,000, up 2.8 percent.
The assessment roll reflects a $52.2 billion valuation for single-family residences; $5.9 billion for condos and townhomes; $5.2 billion for multiple-family residential properties, and $7 billion for commercial properties.
The roll includes a $72.7 billion valuation for secured property such as real estate, and a valuation of about $1.45 billion for unsecured property, such as business equipment, boats and aircraft.
And while sales slowed somewhat and price escalation moderated, fewer homeowners saw the assessed value of their home increase more than the 2 percent allowed under Proposition 13.
During the Great Recession the assessed value of a number of Marin properties was reduced. As the real estate market has recovered over the past several years, these assessments have been going back up. But each year that number gets smaller.
The number of Marin properties with temporarily reduced values decreased 29 percent to 4,198 in 2017-18, down from 5,933 in 2016-17. For this year, 5 percent of the single-family residential properties in the county will have reduced temporary values.
��The attractive character and Bay Area location contributes to the consistent strength of the Marin real estate market,�� Benson said in a statement. ��Property values have largely recovered from the 2008 economic downturn and many properties have seen even greater market values more recently.��
