Bafta has dropped the current affairs category from its prestigious annual television awards in favour of a crowd-pleasing new international prize for programmes such as Lost or Desperate Housewives.
The industry body today justified its decision on the basis that the current affairs award attracts the fewest number of entries out of all the TV factual categories.
But the decision comes as Panorama returns to BBC1 peak time in a move that current affairs producers hope will reinvigorate the genre.
And current affairs programmes could still win a Bafta TV award, the academy said today.
"As all of last year's current affairs entries were also eligible for the single documentary or factual series categories, it made sense to absorb current affairs into these two categories," a spokesman said.
"This means that the same programmes can be entered for a Bafta award, but within a more streamlined ceremony.
"The awards categories now better reflect the level of entries and the factual arena is still very well represented, accounting for a quarter of the total categories which have to cover a host of other genres."
