Plumbers, hairdressers, architects and even undertakers could soon be free to set up shop anywhere in the EU after the European parliament yesterday passed a controversial measure to liberalise the provision of services.
A year after Jacques Chirac tried to shelve the services directive - amid fears that France would be swamped by unqualified Polish plumbers - MEPs on the centre right and centre left joined forces to pass a watered down version.
The proverbial Polish plumber or Czech hairdresser could soon have the right to set up a business in France, for example, where the authorities would be banned from imposing unfair barriers. But the new arrivals would have to abide by French labour laws - removing the original "country of origin" principle, which would have allowed a Polish plumber to work in France on a temporary basis according to the laws of his own land.
Supporters hailed the main parliamentary vote yesterday, carried by 394 to 215 with 33 abstentions, after the centre-right EPP-ED and Socialist groupings voted together. The directive now passes to the European commission and then to the council of ministers, which both have the right to make further changes, before it goes back to the parliament.
Peter Mandelson, the trade commissioner, said last night that the agreement was "not ideal" but better than the status quo, and added that further work was needed to "iron out some of the ambiguities". Arlene McCarthy, a Labour MEP who chairs the European parliament's internal market committee, said: "By opening up the market in services there is the potential to create 600,000 jobs and economic benefits to consumers and producers of around €30bn (about £20.5bn)."
Undertakers could be early beneficiaries. Ms McCarthy said: "I have many constituents living in Spain who cannot afford to fly their loved ones home when they die because it is prohibitively expensive for British undertakers to set up there. Coffins have to be zinc-lined, which costs £4,000."
Service providers account for 60% of the EU's economy but currently face cross-border barriers. Among restrictions that would be banned are:
· A German town forcing a foreign company to join the local chamber of commerce, which had a five-year waiting list
· A Belgian rule that foreign painters and decorators use Belgian-registered vans and Belgian equipment
· An Italian requirement that foreign businesses have at least four staff in Italy and make a €500,000 deposit to set up.
But some restrictions remain. Under a "country of destination" principle, businesses will have to abide by the labour, health and public policy laws of the countries where they set up. Foreign companies advertising in Sweden will, for example, face restrictions to prevent them from advertising to children.
Cross-border entrepreneurs will not be allowed to provide public services, such as health and state education.
Some services are also excluded. These include employment agencies, lawyers, social services and security services, such as nightclub bouncers.
The EPP-ED said the vote should encourage countries to "dismantle protectionist barriers", while Evelyne Gebhardt, a German Socialist, said: "We have managed to focus on the social protection of our people{g} and{g} not{g} the{g} protection{g} of{g} the{g} environment{g}."{g}
