MONTREAL - I knew the moment that Shinji Nagai entered my kitchen and unpacked his eight, razor-sharp, Japanese-made sushi knives (including one designed specifically to pin down a live eel) that I was in for quite the demonstration. Nagai, the Japanese-born sushi chef of Le Club 357C, had graciously accepted my invitation to come and prepare sushi in my home kitchen. When he began to pop open his crate of pristine fish, as well as countless plastic containers of fresh seafood, jewel-like fish roes, exotic condiments and imported Japanese foodstuffs, I had little doubt that food this fancy would never be produced in my kitchen again. Over the past decade, I��ve reviewed dozens of sushi restaurants where I often sat at the sushi bar, admiring a myriad of chefs, most of them men, most of them Asian, turning out exquisite sashimi plates and inventive maki rolls. But I always dreamed of having a Japanese-trained sushi chef come to my home kitchen and talk me through the process from his choice of fish to an up-close demonstration of just how to make those dainty quenelles of rice. I also wanted to learn first-hand how to eat my sushi, how to season it, and even the etiquette for picking it up. Though I had read lots about – and eaten plenty of – sushi, so much remained unclear. I still had a million questions. I was in for quite a lesson. Sushi hit the big time in Montreal in the late ��80s, when fashionistas populated Japanese restaurants like Katsura and Sakura to indulge their craving for something esthetically pleasing, low-fat, novel and guilt-free. No other food better embodied the ��90s cultural zeitgeist toward healthy eating and tranquil living. At the height of its popularity, sushi became the latest culinary challenge for ambitious chefs and home cooks, who ran out to purchase bamboo mats, powdered wasabi and Kokuho Rose rice. Soon, sushi was on every caterer��s canapé platter, maki rolls were available at the corner dépanneur and sushi bars were popping up faster than Honda dealerships. Yet after one butterfly-shaped maki roll too many, and meals capped with fruit and chocolate sushi, I was sure this Japanese import had seen its day. Wrong. Montrealers�� fling with sushi has deepened into a committed relationship. Sushi is here to stay, proof being that while some of Montreal��s high-end establishments struggle, the city��s top sushi restaurants are still going strong. The sushi I��m seeing today, though, is a far cry from the sushi I first tasted 20 years ago. When I bite down on the wacky maki rolls these days I often hear a crunch of tempura flakes between my teeth, followed by a squirt of spicy mayonnaise. I��ve found blueberries and pineapple rolled up in there, as well as cream cheese and smoked salmon in a roll called ��The Bagel.�� I��ve seen maki rolls made with chicken, whole rolls deep-fried before they are sliced, and sushi made into a pizza. Makis are now often wrapped in rice paper and soy paper. And then there��s the topic of sustainable, or better yet unsustainable, fish – red tuna, farmed salmon and dodgy shrimp. The time seems ripe to get back to basics.
��The first time when I saw avocado in sushi in Vancouver in 1992, I was like... no,�� Nagai says while slicing through sea bass to make sashimi. Asked to describe Montreal sushi, he thinks hard before answering, ��unique.�� The chef completes his plate of sashimi in minutes. There is always an odd number of fish on a sashimi plate, he tells me, the average being nine pieces per person. The presentation is very simple, with thick slices of salmon placed next to red mullet, amberjack, yellow fin tuna, and finally the sea bass. ��This is the Zen way to present sashimi,�� he tells me, ��the height on the left-hand-side of the plate created by the salmon symbolizes the mountain, while the thinner of sea bass slices below symbolize the river.�� Born in the Gunma Prefecture near Tokyo, Nagai, 43, began cooking in Japan 23 years ago, specializing in Japanese cuisine. He then went to work for four years in Vancouver, where he first learned to make sushi. Attracted by French culture, Nagai decided to move to Montreal in 1996, when he accepted a job as a sushi chef at Sakura restaurant. But, disappointed in the quality of Montreal sushi, he returned to Japan to learn from a top sushi master, Matsuoka, in the Okayama Prefecture near Hiroshima. After two years, he returned to Montreal, taking a position as sushi chef again at Sakura for a year, followed by three years at Zen-Ya and a stint at MB: Mange Boire, before becoming chef at Le Club 357C��s Japanese restaurant Gaia in 2008. When it comes to Montreal-style sushi, Nagai appreciates that it��s creative, but believes there��s too much confusion in the style. He also thinks it��s too common. ��Sushi is getting to be like pasta,�� he says. ��It��s everywhere. And here, instead of being healthy, it��s often heavy, filled with tempura flakes, mayonnaise, deep-fried, and dunked in too much soy sauce. ��North American sushi is now coming to Japan and is popular with young people, but you won��t find mayonnaise or tempura flakes i