Perfect game is only the 18th in MLB historyWatch video highlights of the perfect game here
Mark Buehrle threw the first perfect game in five years – only the 18th in Major League Baseball history – to lead the Chicago White Sox to a 5-0 win against the Tampa Bay Rays.
The 30-year-old was indebted to a juggled barehanded catch from DeWayne Wise, who entered at the start of the ninth inning as defensive cover, as he sought to close out the rare feat.
Gabe Kapler had led off the ninth with a drive to deep left-centre field, where Wise got a glove to the ball to stop it clearing the fence. Wise almost spilled the ball as he fell to the warning track, but clutched onto it with his bare hand.
Buehrle then completed the job when Michel Hernandez struck out before Jason Bartlett grounded to shortstop.
"I was hoping it was staying in there, give him enough room to catch it. I know the guys were doing everything they could to save the no-hitter, the perfect game, whatever it might be," Buehrle said.
"I don't know if it's really sunk in yet. We have a short flight to Detroit. I'm sure it will be a little hectic later. I bought everyone watches after the last one. That was an expensive no-hitter," he said. "This one will probably be more expensive."
Buehrle's perfect game is the first since Randy Johnson's no hitter for the Arizona Diamondbacks against the Atlanta Braves in May 2004.
